	(text "Stability in ultra-low noise regulators is dictated by the **Output Capacitance ()** and the **Noise Reduction Capacitance ()**. If these ratios are wrong, the LDO will oscillate, turning your \"clean\" power supply into a radio transmitter.\n\nHere are the exact calculated values and part types you need for the **TPS7A94 (Positive)** and **LT3094 (Negative)**, assuming a +/- 6.5V raw supply to get a clean +/- 5.0V output.\n\n### 1. The LDO Component Values (The Critical Stage)\n\nThese regulators do not use standard voltage dividers. They use a precision current source architecture. You must use these specific values to minimize noise density.\n\n#### **Positive Rail: TPS7A94**\n\n* ** (Set Resistor):** **5.0 kΩ** (0.1% tolerance).\n* *Math:* . The internal  is 1mA. .\n\n\n* ** (Output Capacitor):** **2x 22µF** (or 1x 47µF) Ceramic X7R.\n* *Stability:* Must be . Larger is better for lower output impedance.\n* *Part:* Murata GRM32ER71E226KE15 (1210 size avoids DC bias capacitance loss).\n\n\n* ** (Noise Reduction / Soft Start):** **10 µF**.\n* *Function:* Filters the internal reference noise.\n* *Warning:* Do not exceed 22µF, or the startup time becomes excessively slow, potentially causing latch-up issues with the negative rail.\n\n\n\n#### **Negative Rail: LT3094**\n\n* ** (Set Resistor):** **49.9 kΩ** (0.1% tolerance).\n* *Math:* The internal precision current source is 100µA. .\n\n\n* ** (Output Capacitor):** **2x 22µF** (Ceramic X7R).\n* *Stability:* Requires low ESR. Do not use Tantalum here.\n\n\n* ** (Reference Capacitor):** **4.7 µF**.\n* *Function:* This capacitor is parallel to . It kills the thermal noise of the resistor and the reference current shot noise.\n\n\n* ** (Current Limit):** **Left Open** (unless you need to limit current below max).\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Input \"Capacitance Multiplier\" Values (Active Pre-Filter)\n\nThis circuit goes *between* your raw supply (TPS65131) and the LDOs. It smooths the high-frequency switching ripple so the LDO doesn't have to work as hard.\n\n**Constraint:** This circuit drops approx **0.7V to 1.0V**.\n\n* **Required Raw Input:** +/- 6.2V to +/- 6.5V.\n* **Output to LDO:** +/- 5.3V to +/- 5.5V.\n\n#### **Positive Side (NPN Transistor)**\n\n* **Transistor ():** **ZXTN2010A** or generic **BCP56**.\n* *Why:* Medium power, reasonable gain ().\n\n\n* **Base Resistor ():** **1.0 kΩ**.\n* *Function:* Sets the base current and forms the RC filter with .\n\n\n* **Base Capacitor ():** **100 µF** (Electrolytic or Tantalum is fine here).\n* **Protection Diode:** **1N4148** across Base-Emitter (Reverse biased).\n* *Why:* Protects the transistor during power-down if the input collapses faster than the capacitor discharges.\n\n\n\n**The Stability Math:**\n\n* **Cutoff Frequency:** .\n* **Result:** This crushes the 1MHz ripple from your switching regulator by >100dB before it even hits the LDO.\n\n#### **Negative Side (PNP Transistor)**\n\n* **Transistor ():** **ZXTP2012A** or generic **BCP53**.\n* **Base Resistor:** **1.0 kΩ**.\n* **Base Capacitor:** **100 µF**.\n* **Protection Diode:** **1N4148**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Critical Layout & Stability Rules\n\nAt sub-1µV levels, component placement is as important as component value.\n\n1. **NO Ferrite Beads on LDO Outputs:**\n* Never place a ferrite bead directly after the TPS7A94 or LT3094. The inductive reactance will interact with  and cause ringing (oscillation) at 100kHz–1MHz.\n* *Correct placement:* Place ferrite beads **before** the Input Capacitance Multiplier.\n\n\n2. **The \"Star\" Ground:**\n* The \"Ground\" pin of the  resistor and the \"Ground\" pin of the  capacitor must connect to the **Load Ground** (where your op-amp is), *not* the LDO's thermal pad ground.\n* This is called a \"Kelvin Connection.\" If you ground them at the LDO, the current flowing through the PCB trace will create a voltage drop that looks like noise to the regulator.\n\n\n3. **DC Bias Effect:**\n* You are using 5V. If you buy a \"6.3V rated\" 22µF ceramic capacitor, at 5V it will actually only be **~8µF**. This loss of capacitance can destabilize the LDO.\n* **Rule:** Use **25V or 35V rated** capacitors for your 5V rails to ensure they retain their capacitance.\n\n\n\n### Summary Checklist for Ordering\n\n| Component | Value | Dielectric / Type | Rating | Note |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **LDO Output Caps** | 22µF (x2) | X7R Ceramic | **25V+** | Critical for stability |\n| **TPS7A94 ** | 5.0 kΩ | Thin Film | 0.1% | Low Temp Coefficient |\n| **LT3094 ** | 49.9 kΩ | Thin Film | 0.1% | Low Temp Coefficient |\n| **Cap Multiplier Base** | 100 µF | Electrolytic | 16V+ | Lowers ripple freq |\n| **Pass Transistor** | BCP56/53 | SOT-223 | 80V | Handles heat dissipation |\n\nIf you build this specific chain (TPS65131  Cap Multiplier  LDO  Hybrid Op-Amp Rail), you will likely hit the noise floor of your test equipment before you see the noise of the power supply."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
